Thus the approximation-duality handle fixes the correct normalization up to constants, but the frozen assumptions and constant-factor certificates do not establish equality of the liminf and limsup or attainment of an exact leading constant by a computable fixed-sample estimator.","free_symbols":["\(d\)","\(n\)","\(\epsilon\)","\(c_\epsilon\)","\(C_\epsilon\)","\(\mathfrak R_{n,d,\epsilon}\)","\(\mathscr H_\epsilon^{\mathrm{dual}}\)"],"depends_on":["def:minimax-risk","def:exact-constant-handle","thm:sharp-minimax-frontier","prop:l1-reduction"],"status":"to-prove","justification":"This is the strongest exact-normalization conclusion supported by the constant-factor frontier.","gap":"The approximation-duality handle does not itself assert convergence, equality of constants, or a fixed-sample saddle point.","consumer":"A downstream exact-asymptotics analysis now has a proved positive finite bracket and a precise remaining gap."}

For every fixed \(0<\epsilon<1/2\), the answer to \(\mathrm{oeq:four-cell-logarithmic-upper}\) is negative.  There do not exist finite \(C_\epsilon\), positive \(\rho_\epsilon\), and a collection of measurable estimators \(\widehat V_{n,d}\) such that
\[
 \sup_{P\in\mathcal P_{d,\epsilon}}
 E_P\!\left[
  \left\{\widehat V_{n,d}(O_1,\ldots,O_n)-V^\star(P)\right\}^2
 \right]
 \le C_\epsilon\left\{\frac1n+\frac d{n\log(en)}\right\}
\]
for every \(2\le d\le\rho_\epsilon n\log(en)\).  Indeed, for \(d_n=\max\{2,\lfloor\log(en)\rfloor\}\),
\[
 \mathfrak R_{n,d_n,\epsilon}
 \ge \frac{\sqrt{\log(en)}}{8192n}
\]
for all sufficiently large \(n\).  Hence no finite computable four-cell overlap-cone construction can have the proposed uniform risk certificate.  Moreover, along every sequence with \(2\le d\le n^2\), \(\mathfrak R_{n,d,\epsilon}=O(n^{-1})\) holds if and only if \(d=O(1)\); for bounded \(d\), the risk is of order \(n^{-1}\).

